  "name": "King Crimson",
  "editorial_article": "King Crimson is a seminal English progressive rock band founded in London in 1968, renowned for their radical reinventions, intricate compositions, and profound influence on rock, jazz, and avant-garde music. Led by guitarist and founding member Robert Fripp—the group's only continuous member across its 50-plus year history—the band has undergone numerous personnel shifts, each resulting in a distinct musical evolution. Their 1969 debut, 'In the Court of the Crimson King', is widely considered a foundational masterpiece of progressive rock, bridging symphonic art-rock, jazz, and proto-heavy metal with its visceral opener, \"21st Century Schizoid Man.\"\n\nThroughout their career, King Crimson has constantly pushed boundaries, incorporating elements of 20th-century classical, folk, African music, gamelan, free jazz, new wave, and industrial soundscapes. Notable lineups include the early quintet featuring Greg Lake and Ian McDonald, the experimental avant-heavy metal era of the mid-1970s with Bill Bruford and John Wetton (culminating in the acclaimed album 'Red'), and the 1980s new wave-inflected quartet featuring Adrian Belew. Their refusal to stagnate has made them a touchstone for diverse artists, from Kurt Cobain to the band Tool, cementing their legacy as a pioneering force in avant-garde and progressive rock music. Their discography, spanning decades, remains a testament to constant technical innovation and structural exploration, ensuring their status as one of music's most vital and enduring ensembles.",
